1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,487
Coral Reefs: Rainforests of the Sea.

2
00:00:03,437 --> 00:00:15,312
Coral reefs cover less than 1 percent of the ocean floor yet support approximately 25 percent of all marine species — roughly 4,000 fish species and 800 reef-building coral species.

3
00:00:15,312 --> 00:00:27,325
Individual corals are colonies of genetically identical polyps, each typically 1 to 3 millimeters in diameter, that secrete calcium carbonate (CaCO3) exoskeletons.

4
00:00:27,325 --> 00:00:37,162
Over centuries, these accumulated skeletons form reef structures visible from space, such as the Great Barrier Reef, which spans 2,300 kilometers.

5
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:48,112
The foundation of reef productivity is symbiosis with dinoflagellate algae called zooxanthellae, which live within coral tissues at densities exceeding one million cells per square centimeter.

6
00:00:48,112 --> 00:01:00,950
Zooxanthellae perform photosynthesis, supplying the coral host with up to 95 percent of its energy requirements in the form of glucose and amino acids, while receiving carbon dioxide and nitrogenous waste in return.

7
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:07,725
This partnership restricts corals to clear, shallow waters less than 50 meters deep where sunlight penetrates.

8
00:01:07,725 --> 00:01:16,125
When seawater temperatures exceed the long-term summer maximum by 1 to 2 degrees Celsius for several weeks, the symbiosis breaks down.

9
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:23,775
Corals expel their zooxanthellae, revealing the white calcium carbonate skeleton beneath — a phenomenon called coral bleaching.

10
00:01:23,775 --> 00:01:31,412
The 2016 bleaching event on the Great Barrier Reef affected 91 percent of reefs, killing 30 percent of corals.

11
00:01:31,412 --> 00:01:40,012
A 2024 global bleaching event, the fourth recorded since 1998, impacted reefs across 62 countries and territories.

12
00:01:40,012 --> 00:01:54,700
The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change projects that 70 to 90 percent of warm-water corals will disappear if global warming reaches 1.5 degrees Celsius above preindustrial levels, and more than 99 percent at 2 degrees Celsius.

13
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:13,062
Additional threats include ocean acidification — atmospheric CO2 absorption lowers seawater pH, reducing carbonate ion availability and calcification rates by 15 to 25 percent since the Industrial Revolution — and overfishing, which removes herbivorous fish that control algae overgrowth.

14
00:02:13,062 --> 00:02:23,812
Marine protected areas and coral restoration programs, including microfragmentation and larval propagation, currently cover approximately 7 percent of reef areas.

